@@ -74,6 +74,38 @@ llama-server -m Qwen3-4B.gguf -md Qwen3-4B-DFlash.gguf \
 
 `--spec-draft-n-max` is clamped to the draft model's trained block size.
 
+Kimi K3's DSpark checkpoint uses the same runtime with a K3-specific dense
+RoPE-MLA decoder and sequential Markov head. Convert the draft against the
+unquantized target repository so the GGUF gets the target tokenizer and exact
+hidden-state extraction points:
+
+```bash
+python convert_hf_to_gguf.py /models/Inferact-Kimi-K3-DSpark \
+    --target-model-dir /models/moonshotai-Kimi-K3 \
+    --outtype bf16 --outfile Kimi-K3-DSpark-BF16.gguf
+
+llama-server -m Kimi-K3-UD-Q2_K_XL.gguf \
+    -md Kimi-K3-DSpark-BF16.gguf \
+    --spec-type draft-dflash --spec-draft-n-max 3 \
+    --spec-draft-device CUDA1 --spec-draft-ngl all \
+    --temp 0 -fa on --jinja
+```
+
+The target directory only needs its config and tokenizer files; its full
+unquantized weights are not read. Replace `CUDA1` with the device reported by
+`--list-devices`. The K3 checkpoint
+is trained as exactly seven query rows: an anchor and six masks, with the anchor
+predicting the first candidate. Values 3, 5, and 7 for
+`--spec-draft-n-max` only cap how many candidates are returned to the target
+verifier; the draft always evaluates the complete seven-row block. Start with
+3 and sweep 3/5/7 using measured accepted-token throughput.
+
+This K3 path is greedy-only for now (`--temp 0`). It deliberately uses normal
+speculative checkpoints rather than recurrent-state rollback. The converted
+draft shares the target token embedding and output projection at runtime, so
+placing the target and draft on different RPC devices still needs an
+end-to-end backend compatibility and transfer-cost measurement.
+
 See:
 
 - #22105
